# Copy to .env.local and fill in. PLACEHOLDERS ONLY in this file — never commit
# a real value here. Next.js loads .env.local from this directory; db/reset.sh
# and the psql scripts read the same variables from the shell.
#
# For local development you do not need this file at all: `dev.env` supplies
# explicit non-secret development values, and both db/reset.sh and `npm run
# test` source it. Use .env.local when you want values of your own.

# --- Identity peppers — REQUIRED, no fallback exists -------------------------
# The application refuses to hash a phone number if either of these is missing,
# empty, under 32 characters, or set to a known placeholder. That is deliberate:
# a forgotten pepper used to mean a working app with a publicly-known HMAC key
# and no visible symptom.
#
#   FITOS_ORG_HASH_SECRET  derives a per-organization key, so two retailers
#                          holding the same person produce different hashes and
#                          the platform cannot correlate customers across
#                          retailers as a side effect of the schema.
#   FITOS_IDENTITY_SECRET  the global key behind identity_lookup_hash, used only
#                          inside identity resolution.
#
# Generate each with:  openssl rand -base64 48
#
# CHANGING EITHER VALUE INVALIDATES EVERY HASH DERIVED FROM IT. On a database
# with real fittings, existing customers stop being findable by phone. See
# docs/05, "Identity peppers", for the reseed-or-rotate decision.
FITOS_ORG_HASH_SECRET=replace-with-random-48-byte-base64-value
FITOS_IDENTITY_SECRET=replace-with-a-different-random-48-byte-base64-value

# --- Postgres connection ----------------------------------------------------
# The application connects as a role WITHOUT BYPASSRLS and switches to it per
# transaction. Migrations and seeding run as a separate service role.
PGHOST=127.0.0.1
PGPORT=5432
PGUSER=replace-with-app-role
PGPASSWORD=replace-with-password
PGDATABASE=replace-with-database-name
